0 = 4e^{t} - e^{4t} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigl(4e^{t}-e^{4t}\\bigr)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nα_{1}t + α_{0} &= (\\frac{1}{3t}(e^{4t} - e^{t}))t + \\frac{1}{3}(4e^{t} - e^{4t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(e^{4t} - e^{t} + 4e^{t} - e^{4t}) \\\\\n&= e^{t} \\\\\n- 3α_{1}t &= -3(\\frac{1}{3t}(e^{4t} - e^{t}))t \\\\\n&= e^{t} - e^{4t} \\\\\n4α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 4(\\frac{1}{3t}(e^{4t} - e^{t}))t + \\frac{1}{3}(4e^{t} - e^{4t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}((4e^{4t} - 4e^{t}) + (4e^{t} - e^{4t})) \\\\\n&= e^{4t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} & 0 \\\\ e^{t} - e^{4t} & e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535191 false] [17592186221091 1145 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=4t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ r(4t)={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3t\\alpha_1 = e^{4t} - e^{t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_1 = \\dfrac{1}{3t}\\bigl(e^{4t}-e^{t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $4$ and subtracting the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3\\alpha_0 = 4e^{t} - e^{4t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigl(4e^{t}-e^{4t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}+4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t} \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-{e}^{4t} \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((4{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{t})+(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t})) \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/104a4830d44821dbc65ad9c832da0323b7dd9e90c934fdfc7a21c175845231d2.png)" 13194139535191 true] [17592186221092 1100 "Find $e^{At}$ for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/3f97cbbd4715d8191dc5b0c410a784b556b068ed5e3e57fe0dc16ee8f52655cc.png) \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $3 \\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$*" 13194139535191 true] [17592186221092 1100 "Find $e^{At}$ for $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $3 \\pm 2\\textrm{i}$.*" 13194139535191 false] [17592186221092 1145 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}7α_{1}t + α_{0} & 4α_{1}t \\\\ -5α_{1}t & - α_{1}t + α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues or $At$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= (3+2\\textrm{i}) t, \\qquad \\lambda_2= (3-2\\textrm{i}) t\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr((3 + 2\\textrm{i})t) &= e^{(3 + 2\\textrm{i})t} \\\\\nα_{1}(3 + 2\\textrm{i})t + α_{0} &= e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n(3α_{1}t + α_{0}) + 2α_{1}\\textrm{i}t &= e^{3t}\\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}e^{3t}\\sin (2t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n{3α_{1}t + α_{0} = e^{3t}\\cos (2t); 2tα_{1} = e^{3t}\\sin (2t)⇒{α_{1} = \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t); α_{0} = e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}\\sin (2t))\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t + e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) + e^{3t}\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\\\\n&= e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) + 2\\sin (2t)), \\\\\n4α_{1}t &= 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t \\\\\n&= 2e^{3t}\\sin (2t), \\\\\n- 5α_{1}t &= -5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t), \\\\\n- α_{1}t + α_{0} &= -\\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t + e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) + e^{3t}\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\\\\n&= e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - 2\\sin (2t))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) + 2\\sin (2t) & 2\\sin (2t) \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}\\sin (2t) & \\cos (2t) - 2\\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535191 false] [17592186221092 1145 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= (3+2\\textrm{i}) t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2= (3-2\\textrm{i}) t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((3+2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(3+2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(3+2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ (3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+2{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)), \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot 