nd $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n$\\alpha_1 = e^{6t}$.\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n$6t e^{6t} + \\alpha_0 = e^{6t} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\alpha_0 = e^{6t}(1-6t)$.\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 8e^{6t}t + e^{6t}(1 - 6t) \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(1 + 2t), \\\\\n- 2α_{1}t &= -2e^{6t}t \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(-2t), \\\\\n2α_{1}t &= 2e^{6t}t \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(2t), \\\\\n4α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 4e^{6t}t + e^{6t}(1 - 6t) \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(1 - 2t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 2t & -2t \\\\ 2t & 1 - 2t\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535191 false] [17592186221093 1145 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 6t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(6t)={e}^{6t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(6t)={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 = e^{6t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n6t e^{6t} + \\alpha_0 = e^{6t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = e^{6t}(1-6t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_